[
  {
    "chunk_id": "faraday_001",
    "series": "Series I",
    "paragraphs": "§1–6",
    "year": 1831,
    "topic": "Electromagnetic Induction — Iron Ring Discovery",
    "text": "In the autumn of 1831 Faraday wound two coils of wire around a soft iron ring 'seven-eighths of an inch thick' and 'six inches' in external diameter. Coil A consisted of three separate lengths of copper wire, each about 24 feet long, helically wound and insulated with twine and calico. Coil B consisted of two lengths, each about 60 feet long. Coil A was connected to a voltaic battery of one hundred pairs of plates; coil B was connected to a galvanometer needle. On completing the battery circuit, the galvanometer needle was deflected and then returned to its original position. On breaking the circuit, it was again deflected — but in the opposite direction — and again returned to zero. No deflection occurred while the battery current was flowing steadily. Faraday noted in §6 that 'the effects on the needle were not permanent, but were merely indications of the sudden change in the electromagnetic condition of the coil at the moments of making and breaking the circuit.' This transient character was fundamental: induction requires change, not a steady state.",
    "source": "Experimental Researches in Electricity, Volume I (Project Gutenberg #14986)",
    "concepts": ["electromagnetic induction", "transient current", "iron ring", "galvanometer", "Series I"],
    "difficulty": "hard"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "faraday_002",
    "series": "Series I",
    "paragraphs": "§10–11",
    "year": 1831,
    "topic": "Induction by a Changing Current — Helix Experiment",
    "text": "In §10 Faraday wound 'two hundred and three feet' of copper wire into a spiral helix and connected it to a galvanometer. A straight wire carrying a voltaic current was placed parallel to this helix. On starting or stopping the primary current a transient deflection of the galvanometer needle was observed, but no deflection occurred while the primary current flowed steadily. This showed that a permanent magnet was not necessary: any changing current produced an inductive effect. Faraday noted in §11 that the direction of the deflection was opposite when the circuit was made versus broken. The key principle he drew was that flux change — not flux itself — was the operative condition for electromagnetic induction.",
    "source": "Experimental Researches in Electricity, Volume I (Project Gutenberg #14986)",
    "concepts": ["helix", "203 feet of wire", "flux change", "transient deflection", "Series I"],
    "difficulty": "hard"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "faraday_003",
    "series": "Series I",
    "paragraphs": "§17–26",
    "year": 1831,
    "topic": "Bar Magnet Induction — Motion Required",
    "text": "In §17 Faraday thrust a cylindrical bar magnet 'twelve inches long' into 'a helix of two hundred and twenty turns' wound on a hollow pasteboard cylinder. While the magnet was at rest inside the coil, the galvanometer showed no deflection whatsoever. The moment the magnet was pushed in, a transient deflection appeared; on withdrawing it, the deflection was in the opposite direction. Moving the magnet faster produced a stronger deflection. In §26 Faraday concluded: 'a conductor must cut across the magnetic curves of force to generate an electromotive force.' The position of the magnet was irrelevant; only the act of cutting was decisive.",
    "source": "Experimental Researches in Electricity, Volume I (Project Gutenberg #14986)",
    "concepts": ["bar magnet", "12 inches long", "220-turn helix", "cutting lines of force", "relative motion", "Series I"],
    "difficulty": "hard"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "faraday_004",
    "series": "Series I",
    "paragraphs": "§31",
    "year": 1831,
    "topic": "Power of One Hundred Pairs — Needle Spinning",
    "text": "In §31 Faraday recorded a dramatic quantitative experiment: 'Upon using the power of one hundred pairs of plates (§10.) with this ring, the impulse at the galvanometer, when contact was completed or broken, was so great as to make the needle spin round rapidly four or five times, before the air and terrestrial magnetism could reduce its motion to mere oscillation.' This was a direct measure of the strength of the induced current generated by the iron-ring apparatus. The spinning of the needle four or five times indicated a very large induced charge pulse — far greater than Faraday had produced in earlier induction experiments with smaller batteries.",
    "source": "Experimental Researches in Electricity, Volume I (Project Gutenberg #14986)",
    "concepts": ["100 pairs of plates", "galvanometer needle", "spinning 4-5 times", "induction strength", "Series I"],
    "difficulty": "hard"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "faraday_005",
    "series": "Series I",
    "paragraphs": "§34–44",
    "year": 1831,
    "topic": "Direction of Induced Current — Lenz's Law Precursor",
    "text": "In §34 Faraday determined the direction of the induced current by observing galvanometer deflections systematically. He established that when a primary current was established in coil A, the induced current in coil B flowed 'in the opposite direction to that which the primary combination would have produced.' When the primary current was broken, the induced current was in the same direction as the primary. He verified this with both the iron-ring apparatus and the bar-magnet helix. In §44 he tabulated the results systematically. These observations were later codified by Lenz (1834) as: the induced current always opposes the change that produces it — what we now call Lenz's Law. Faraday's exact phrase in §34 is: 'in the opposite direction to that which the primary combination would have produced.'",
    "source": "Experimental Researches in Electricity, Volume I (Project Gutenberg #14986)",
    "concepts": ["direction of induced current", "opposite direction", "Lenz's Law precursor", "§34 exact phrase", "Series I"],
    "difficulty": "hard"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "faraday_006",
    "series": "Series I",
    "paragraphs": "§55–71",
    "year": 1831,
    "topic": "Copper Disc Generator — First Continuous EMF",
    "text": "In §55–71 Faraday described the construction of the first practical electromagnetic generator: a copper disc 'twelve inches in diameter', rotated between the poles of a large horseshoe magnet. Electrical contact was made at the rim and at the axle of the disc. When the disc was rotated at a steady speed, a continuous (non-transient) electromotive force was produced — unlike all previous induction effects, which had been momentary. He verified the output using both a galvanometer deflection and a chemical decomposition test. The current was proportional to the rotational speed of the disc. He called it 'a new electrical machine' and noted that it differed from all earlier frictional generators in producing a continuous current without any commutator. He also noted that a frog was powerfully convulsed by the output of this machine.",
    "source": "Experimental Researches in Electricity, Volume I (Project Gutenberg #14986)",
    "concepts": ["copper disc", "12 inches diameter", "horseshoe magnet", "continuous EMF", "homopolar generator", "Series I"],
    "difficulty": "medium"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "faraday_007",
    "series": "Series II",
    "paragraphs": "§113",
    "year": 1832,
    "topic": "Bar Magnet Dimensions for Magneto-Electric Experiments",
    "text": "In §113 of Series II, Faraday noted that although the revolving-plate experiments were first performed with the large magnet of the Royal Society, they were 'ultimately repeated with a couple of bar magnets two feet long, one inch and a half wide, and half an inch thick'. Using these smaller bar magnets and a more sensitive galvanometer, the results were obtained 'with the most striking results.' This demonstrates that magneto-electric induction did not require exceptional apparatus: a pair of standard bar magnets of known dimensions (2 feet × 1.5 inches × 0.5 inch) sufficed to produce clear and repeatable induced currents.",
    "source": "Experimental Researches in Electricity, Volume I (Project Gutenberg #14986)",
    "concepts": ["bar magnets", "2 feet long", "1.5 inches wide", "0.5 inch thick", "magneto-electric induction", "Series II"],
    "difficulty": "hard"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "faraday_008",
    "series": "Series II",
    "paragraphs": "§188–189",
    "year": 1832,
    "topic": "Waterloo Bridge Experiment — Tidal Electricity",
    "text": "In §188–189, Faraday attempted to detect electricty generated by the tidal flow of the Thames. He extended 'a copper wire nine hundred and sixty feet in length upon the parapet of the bridge' (Waterloo Bridge), dropping from its extremities other wires with extensive plates of metal into the water to complete contact. 'Thus the wire and the water made one conducting circuit; and as the water ebbed or flowed with the tide, I hoped to obtain currents analogous to those of the brass ball (§161.).' He constantly obtained deflections at the galvanometer, 'but they were very irregular, and were, in succession, referred to other causes than that sought for' — differences in water purity, temperature, and plate conditions. After three days of experiments he could not obtain satisfactory results, though he theoretically argued that such tidal electric currents must exist.",
    "source": "Experimental Researches in Electricity, Volume I (Project Gutenberg #14986)",
    "concepts": ["Waterloo Bridge", "960 feet of wire", "tidal electricity", "Thames", "inconclusive result", "Series II"],
    "difficulty": "hard"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "faraday_009",
    "series": "Series III",
    "paragraphs": "§265–307",
    "year": 1833,
    "topic": "Identity of Electricities — Common and Voltaic",
    "text": "In Series III (read January 10th and 17th, 1833) Faraday undertook to prove that electricity produced by an electrical machine (common electricity) and electricity produced by a voltaic battery were identical in nature. He compared them by five effects: (I) Tension — both showed gold-leaf repulsion; (II) Magnetism — both deflected the galvanometer needle equally; (III) Chemical decomposition — both decomposed water and salts in the same proportions; (IV) Physiological effects — both produced shocks, tongue sensations, and eye flashes; (V) Spark — both produced sparks. In §307 he concluded: 'From all these experiments, it appears that a current of common electricity... is still able to deflect the needle; the only requisite being, apparently, to allow time for its action: that it is, in fact, just as magnetic as voltaic electricity.' Faraday presented a summary table in §360 showing all five electricities (voltaic, common, thermo-, magneto-, animal) producing identical effects.",
    "source": "Experimental Researches in Electricity, Volume I (Project Gutenberg #14986)",
    "concepts": ["identity of electricities", "five effects", "voltaic electricity", "common electricity", "Series III"],
    "difficulty": "medium"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "faraday_010",
    "series": "Series III",
    "paragraphs": "§371",
    "year": 1832,
    "topic": "Quantitative Measurement of Electricity — Platina-Zinc Wire",
    "text": "In §371 Faraday described a key quantitative experiment for comparing voltaic electricity with Leyden-jar electricity. 'A platina and a zinc wire were passed through the same hole of a draw-plate, being then one eighteenth of an inch in diameter; these were fastened to a support, so that their lower ends projected, were parallel, and five sixteenths of an inch apart.' They were 'immersed to the depth of five eighths of an inch in acid, consisting of one drop oil of vitriol and four ounces distilled water, at a temperature about 60 deg.' The other extremities were connected by 'a copper wire eighteen feet long, and one-eighteenth of an inch in thickness.' Faraday stated that this arrangement 'yielded as much electricity in little more than three seconds of time as a Leyden battery charged by thirty turns of a very large and powerful plate electric machine in full action.'",
    "source": "Experimental Researches in Electricity, Volume I (Project Gutenberg #14986)",
    "concepts": ["platina wire", "zinc wire", "1/18 inch diameter", "5/16 inch apart", "5/8 inch depth", "one drop vitriol four ounces water", "18-foot copper wire", "Series III"],
    "difficulty": "hard"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "faraday_011",
    "series": "Series IV",
    "paragraphs": "§380–395",
    "year": 1833,
    "topic": "New Law of Electric Conduction — Ice Non-Conductor",
    "text": "Series IV (received April 24, read May 23, 1833) opens with Faraday's unexpected discovery that ice is an electrical non-conductor. 'I was working with ice... when I was suddenly stopped in my progress by finding that ice was in such circumstances a non-conductor of electricity; and that as soon as a thin film of it was interposed, in the circuit of a very powerful voltaic battery, the transmission of electricity was prevented, and all decomposition ceased' (§381). He repeated the experiments many times, using batteries up to 'fifteen troughs, or one hundred and fifty pairs of four-inch plates, powerfully charged', yet 'no sensible quantity of electricity passed the thin barrier of ice' (§389). In §394 he generalised: 'As it did not seem likely that this law of the assumption of conducting power during liquefaction, and loss of it during congelation, would be peculiar to water, I immediately proceeded to ascertain its influence in other cases.' In §395 he fused chloride of lead and found it conducted; the law was general.",
    "source": "Experimental Researches in Electricity, Volume I (Project Gutenberg #14986)",
    "concepts": ["ice non-conductor", "law of liquefaction", "chloride of lead", "150 plates", "Series IV"],
    "difficulty": "hard"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "faraday_012",
    "series": "Series IV",
    "paragraphs": "§432–435",
    "year": 1833,
    "topic": "Sulphuret of Silver — Heat Increases Conducting Power",
    "text": "In §432–435 Faraday described 'an extraordinary case' of heat increasing electrical conducting power: sulphuret of silver (silver sulphide, Ag₂S). 'The substance presenting this effect is sulphuret of silver. It was made by fusing a mixture of precipitated silver and sublimed sulphur, removing the film of silver by a file from the exterior of the fused mass, pulverizing the sulphuret, mingling it with more sulphur, and fusing it again in a green glass tube, so that no air should obtain access during the operation.' When cold, the material was a poor conductor. When heated (still well below melting point), its conducting power increased dramatically — the opposite of metallic conductors, where heat increases resistance. Faraday noted (§432) this was 'in direct contrast with the influence of heat upon metallic bodies, as observed and described by Sir Humphry Davy.' This was the first documented semiconductor-like behaviour.",
    "source": "Experimental Researches in Electricity, Volume I (Project Gutenberg #14986)",
    "concepts": ["sulphuret of silver", "Ag2S", "semiconductor", "heat increases conductivity", "negative temperature coefficient", "Series IV"],
    "difficulty": "hard"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "faraday_013",
    "series": "Series VII",
    "paragraphs": "§783–821",
    "year": 1834,
    "topic": "Law of Definite Electrolytic Action",
    "text": "Series VII (received January 9, read January–February 1834) contains Faraday's definitive proof of his law of electrolysis. He had first stated the law in Series III: 'the chemical power of a current of electricity is in direct proportion to the absolute quantity of electricity which passes' (§783, referencing §377). In Series VII he proved this by connecting a zinc voltameter and a silver voltameter in series — the same absolute quantity of electricity must pass through both. He showed that the masses of zinc and silver deposited were in the same ratio as their chemical combining weights (electrochemical equivalents). In §821 he concluded: 'All these facts combine into, I think, an irresistible mass of evidence, proving the truth of the important proposition... that the chemical power of a current of electricity is in direct proportion to the absolute quantity of electricity which passes.' He further proved (§821) that this law holds 'not merely true with one substance, as water, but generally with all electrolytic bodies... the whole combining together into one series of definite electro-chemical actions.'",
    "source": "Experimental Researches in Electricity, Volume I (Project Gutenberg #14986)",
    "concepts": ["law of definite electrolytic action", "Faraday's first law", "electrochemical equivalents", "combining weights", "Series VII"],
    "difficulty": "medium"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "faraday_014",
    "series": "Series VII",
    "paragraphs": "§861–862",
    "year": 1834,
    "topic": "Quantitative Electricity and Atomic Charge — 800,000 Leyden Charges",
    "text": "In §861 Faraday made a remarkable quantitative calculation about the electricity associated with matter: 'it would appear that 800,000 such charges of the Leyden battery as I have referred to above, would be necessary to supply electricity sufficient to decompose a single grain of water; or, if I am right, to equal the quantity of electricity which is naturally associated with the elements of that grain of water, endowing them with their mutual chemical affinity.' This was an estimate of the enormous amount of electricity latent in even a tiny mass of matter. In §862 he called the experiment to prove this 'an experiment of great simplicity but extreme beauty, when viewed in relation to the evolution of an electric current and its decomposing powers.' The Leyden battery in question was one charged by 'thirty turns of a very large and powerful plate electric machine in full action' (§371).",
    "source": "Experimental Researches in Electricity, Volume I (Project Gutenberg #14986)",
    "concepts": ["800000 Leyden charges", "one grain of water", "atomic charge", "electrochemical power", "Series VII"],
    "difficulty": "hard"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "faraday_015",
    "series": "Series VII",
    "paragraphs": "§864–868",
    "year": 1834,
    "topic": "Zinc Plate Weighing Experiment — Electrochemical Equivalent of Zinc",
    "text": "In §864 Faraday described the precise weighing experiment that established the electrochemical equivalent of zinc. 'Two plates of zinc thus amalgamated were dried and accurately weighed; one, which we will call A, weighed 163.1 grains; the other, to be called B, weighed 148.3 grains. They were about five inches long, and 0.4 of an inch wide.' Plate A was connected to a voltaic circuit and dissolved in acid while plate B was kept inactive as a control. After the experiment, A was reweighed to determine the exact mass of zinc dissolved. In §868 Faraday concluded from the results: 'the chemical action upon 32.31 parts, or one equivalent of zinc, in this simple voltaic arrangement, was attended by the evolution of the same quantity of electricity as 800,000 of the charges of the Leyden battery'. Thus the electrochemical equivalent of zinc was established as 32.31 parts (grains).",
    "source": "Experimental Researches in Electricity, Volume I (Project Gutenberg #14986)",
    "concepts": ["zinc plates", "163.1 grains", "148.3 grains", "5 inches long", "0.4 inch wide", "32.31 equivalent", "Series VII"],
    "difficulty": "hard"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "faraday_016",
    "series": "Series IX",
    "paragraphs": "§1048–1064",
    "year": 1835,
    "topic": "Self-Induction Discovery — Jenkin's Observation and 132-Foot Wire",
    "text": "Series IX (received December 18, 1834; read January 29, 1835) opens with the discovery of self-induction. In §1049 Faraday described the crucial observation communicated to him by 'Mr. Jenkin': if an ordinary short wire connects a single pair of metals, no shock is obtained; but if the wire surrounding an electro-magnet is used, a shock is felt each time the contact is broken. This was the first observation of what we now call the back-EMF of an inductor. In §1064 Faraday investigated self-induction in a plain long wire: 'A copper wire, 0.18 of an inch in diameter, and 132 feet in length, was laid out upon the floor of the laboratory, and used as the connecting conductor.' On making contact no sensible spark appeared, but on breaking contact 'a bright one' appeared. Furthermore, 'by using two silver plates fastened by small wires to the extremity of the principal wire used, and introducing the tongue between those plates, I succeeded in obtaining powerful shocks upon the tongue and gums, and could easily convulse a flounder, an eel, or a frog.'",
    "source": "Experimental Researches in Electricity, Volume I (Project Gutenberg #14986)",
    "concepts": ["self-induction", "Jenkin observation", "132-foot copper wire", "0.18 inch diameter", "back-EMF", "Series IX"],
    "difficulty": "hard"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "faraday_017",
    "series": "Series IX",
    "paragraphs": "§1096",
    "year": 1835,
    "topic": "Doubled Wire — No Self-Induction Spark",
    "text": "In §1096 Faraday demonstrated the directional nature of self-induction by folding a wire back on itself. 'A wire forty feet long, covered with silk, being doubled and tied closely together to within four inches of the extremities, when used in that state, gave scarcely a perceptible spark; but being opened out and the parts separated, it gave a very good one.' This proved that self-induction was a consequence of the magnetic field built up by the current, not simply of the wire's resistance or length: when two halves of the wire carried current in opposite directions, their magnetic effects cancelled and no self-induction occurred. When the wire was straightened so both halves carried current in the same direction, the full self-inductive spark appeared.",
    "source": "Experimental Researches in Electricity, Volume I (Project Gutenberg #14986)",
    "concepts": ["doubled wire", "no spark", "self-induction cancellation", "40-foot wire", "magnetic field", "Series IX"],
    "difficulty": "hard"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "faraday_018",
    "series": "Series XI",
    "paragraphs": "§1161–1168",
    "year": 1837,
    "topic": "Opening of Series XI — Induction as Universal Principle",
    "text": "Series XI (received November 30, read December 21, 1837) opens with one of the most eloquent statements in Faraday's Researches. In §1161: 'The science of electricity is in that state in which every part of it requires experimental investigation; not merely for the discovery of new effects, but what is just now of far more importance, the development of the means by which the old effects are produced, and the consequent more accurate determination of the first principles of action of the most extraordinary and universal power in nature.' Faraday stated in §1162 that of all electrical phenomena, 'none... excels, or even equals in importance, that called Induction. It is of the most general influence in electrical phenomena, appearing to be concerned in every one of them, and has in reality the character of a first, essential, and fundamental principle.' In §1165 he proposed a new theory: that electric induction is an action of contiguous particles of the insulating medium (the dielectric), which he proposed to call the dielectric.",
    "source": "Experimental Researches in Electricity, Volume I (Project Gutenberg #14986)",
    "concepts": ["induction as universal principle", "dielectric", "contiguous particles", "Series XI opening", "Series XI"],
    "difficulty": "medium"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "faraday_019",
    "series": "Series XI",
    "paragraphs": "§1218–1224",
    "year": 1837,
    "topic": "Shell-Lac Cylinder — Induction in Curved Lines",
    "text": "In §1218 Faraday described a key experiment for proving that electric induction follows curved paths (not simply straight lines as assumed under action-at-a-distance theory). 'A cylinder of solid shell-lac, 0.9 of an inch in diameter and seven inches in length, was fixed upright in a wooden foot; it was made concave or cupped at its upper extremity so that a brass ball or other small arrangement could stand upon it.' The upper half of the stem was excited negatively by friction with warm flannel. He then used a carrier ball to measure the induced charge at various angular positions around the stem, finding in §1224 that the inductive force existed at positions not directly above the excited surface — at positions _k_, _l_, _m_, _n_ — proving that lines of inductive force followed curved paths through the air around the charged body.",
    "source": "Experimental Researches in Electricity, Volume I (Project Gutenberg #14986)",
    "concepts": ["shell-lac cylinder", "0.9 inch diameter", "7 inches long", "induction in curved lines", "carrier ball", "Series XI"],
    "difficulty": "hard"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "faraday_020",
    "series": "Series XI",
    "paragraphs": "§1252–1260",
    "year": 1838,
    "topic": "Specific Inductive Capacity — Shell-Lac Measurement",
    "text": "The Supplementary Note to Series XI (received March 29, 1838) contains Faraday's precise measurement of the specific inductive capacity (SIC) of shell-lac relative to air. Using two inductive apparatus hemispheres (app. i. with shell-lac, app. ii. with air), he charged app. i. and divided the charge with app. ii. The measured result: 'a transferable charge in app. ii. of 469 deg. was divided with app. i.' yielding unequal portions, from which the SIC of shell-lac was computed as the ratio 176/113 = 1.558 relative to air (§1260). 'This extraordinary difference was so unexpected in its amount, as to excite the greatest suspicion of the general accuracy of the experiment, though the perfect discharge of app. i. after the division, showed that the 113 deg. had been taken and given up readily.' He verified the result in the reverse direction (charging the shell-lac apparatus first), obtaining consistent results.",
    "source": "Experimental Researches in Electricity, Volume I (Project Gutenberg #14986)",
    "concepts": ["specific inductive capacity", "shell-lac", "176/113 = 1.558", "inductive apparatus", "dielectric constant", "Series XI"],
    "difficulty": "hard"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "faraday_021",
    "series": "Series XI",
    "paragraphs": "§1275–1280",
    "year": 1838,
    "topic": "Specific Inductive Capacity — Sulphur Measurement",
    "text": "In §1275 Faraday reported the SIC of sulphur using the same inductive apparatus. He first charged the air apparatus (app. i.) to 'Balls 280 deg.' and then divided the charge with the sulphur apparatus (app. ii.). After division, the sulphur apparatus retained '162 deg.' while the air apparatus showed the corresponding remainder. This measurement (§1275) indicated that sulphur had a higher specific inductive capacity than air, with a value approximately 2.24 relative to air — higher than the shell-lac value of 1.558. The sulphur hemisphere 'was free from charge both before and after each experiment, and no action from the stem appeared (§1203. §1232.), so that no correction was required on that account.' The finding that different solid insulators had distinctly different dielectric constants was entirely new: previous electrical theory had assumed all insulators were equivalent.",
    "source": "Experimental Researches in Electricity, Volume I (Project Gutenberg #14986)",
    "concepts": ["specific inductive capacity", "sulphur", "280 degrees to 162 degrees", "approximately 2.24", "dielectric constant", "Series XI"],
    "difficulty": "hard"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "faraday_022",
    "series": "Series XI",
    "paragraphs": "§1295",
    "year": 1838,
    "topic": "General Result — Induction as Action of Contiguous Particles",
    "text": "In §1295 Faraday stated his central conclusion about the nature of electric induction: 'Thus induction appears to be essentially an action of contiguous particles, through the intermediation of which the electric force, originating or appearing at a certain place, is propagated to or sustained at a distance, appearing there as a force of the same kind exactly equal in amount, but opposite in its direction and tendencies (§1164.).' He noted that 'Induction requires no sensible thickness in the conductors which may be used to limit its extent, but it does require that the whole of the space between the inductric and inducteous surfaces shall be occupied by a dielectric.' This contiguous-particle view of induction was his alternative to the Newtonian action-at-a-distance theories of Poisson and others, and it directly inspired Maxwell's field theory of electromagnetism.",
    "source": "Experimental Researches in Electricity, Volume I (Project Gutenberg #14986)",
    "concepts": ["contiguous particles", "induction propagation", "dielectric", "§1295 key statement", "Series XI", "Maxwell field theory precursor"],
    "difficulty": "medium"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "faraday_023",
    "series": "Series XII",
    "paragraphs": "§1318–1370",
    "year": 1838,
    "topic": "Conduction as Extreme Induction — Insulators and Conductors on a Continuum",
    "text": "In §1318 (Series XII, received January 11, read February 8, 1838) Faraday proposed that conduction and induction are not fundamentally different phenomena but differ only in degree. In §1320 he wrote: 'both induction and conduction appear to be the same in their principle and action, except that in the latter an effect common to both is raised to the highest degree, whereas in the former it occurs in the best cases, in only an almost insensible quantity.' In §1328 he illustrated this with an extreme case: a sulphur block (a near-perfect insulator) still allows a tiny amount of inductive leakage; a metallic wire (a near-perfect conductor) still has some resistance. In §1334 he concluded: 'the relation of conductors and non-conductors has been shown to be one not of opposition in kind, but only of degree.' He measured the insulating power of specific gases in §1388 and presented a 'Table of discharge in gases', noting that gases under different pressures insulated with different efficiencies.",
    "source": "Experimental Researches in Electricity, Volume I (Project Gutenberg #14986)",
    "concepts": ["conduction = induction in degree", "continuum of insulators and conductors", "§1320", "Series XII"],
    "difficulty": "medium"
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "faraday_024",
    "series": "Series XIV",
    "paragraphs": "§1667–1709",
    "year": 1838,
    "topic": "Nature of Electric Force — Distribution, Not Identity",
    "text": "Series XIV (received and read June 21, 1838) opens with §1667: 'The theory of induction set forth and illustrated in the three preceding series of experimental researches does not assume anything new as to the nature of the electric force or forces, but only as to their distribution.' Faraday explicitly stated that his induction theory was neutral between the one-fluid theory of Franklin, Cavendish and Mossotti, the two-fluid theory of Dufay and Poisson, and vibration theories — any of these was compatible with his distribution-based view. In §1668 he emphasised: 'to ascertain how the forces are arranged, to trace them in their various relations to the particles of matter, to determine their general laws, and also the specific differences which distinguish the action of one body from that of another — this is the object of all experimental research in electricity.' In §1690 he described a precise apparatus: 'A brass ball 0.73 of an inch in diameter, fixed at the end of a horizontal brass rod' used to investigate induction through crystals.",
    "source": "Experimental Researches in Electricity, Volume I (Project Gutenberg #14986)",
    "concepts": ["nature of electric force", "one-fluid vs two-fluid theory", "0.73 inch brass ball", "induction through crystals", "Series XIV"],
