    "topic": "Relation of Electric and Magnetic Forces — Field Lines",
    "text": "In §1709 (Series XIV, S21) Faraday addressed the deep question of the relation between electric and magnetic forces. He proposed that both might act through the intermediate particles of matter, as electric induction does, rather than at a distance through empty space. He constructed two magneto-electric helices with iron cores separated by 'an interval of an inch and three quarters' (§1711) and showed that moving a bar magnet between them caused currents in both helices. In §1729 he stated that 'the magnetic forces are active through the intervening particles' and in §1731 he concluded that 'the magnetic forces active through intervening particles, in analogy with the induction of static electricity, or the various effects, such as conduction, discharge, &c., which are dependent on that induction.' In §1735 he noted that 'magnetic forces act at a distance, and if not through the mediation of the intervening particles, have their relations to ordinary matter entirely unlike those of the electrical forces.' This laid the conceptual groundwork for Maxwell's unified field theory.",
