  {
    "chunk_id": "faraday_026",
    "series": "Series XIX (Volume III)",
    "paragraphs": "§2146–2242",
    "year": 1845,
    "topic": "Magnetic Action on Light — Faraday Effect (Magneto-Optical Rotation)",
    "text": "On September 13, 1845, Faraday discovered the magneto-optical effect now called the Faraday Effect. Working with a dense glass rod placed along the lines of magnetic force of a powerful electromagnet, he found that when polarised light passed through the glass rod parallel to the field, its plane of polarisation was rotated. The rotation was in the direction of the positive pole on the side from which the light emerged. The effect was observed in many transparent solids and liquids but not in gases with his apparatus. The angle of rotation was found to be proportional to the magnetic field strength and to the length of medium traversed. He called this the 'magnetisation of light' and saw it as direct evidence of a connection between magnetism and light, writing: 'I have at last succeeded in illuminating a magnetic curve or line of force, and in magnetising a ray of light.' This work is in Series XIX of the Experimental Researches (Volume III), not Volume I.",
    "source": "Experimental Researches in Electricity, Volume III (Series XIX, 1845)",
    "concepts": ["Faraday effect", "magneto-optical rotation", "plane of polarisation", "heavy glass rod", "proportional to field and length", "Series XIX"],
