  {
    "chunk_id": "faraday_027",
    "series": "Series XIX (Volume III)",
    "paragraphs": "§2243–2350",
    "year": 1845,
    "topic": "Diamagnetics — Bismuth Repulsion and New Class of Substances",
    "text": "In the same series of 1845 work, Faraday discovered that bismuth and many other non-magnetic substances were repelled by both poles of a strong magnet — the opposite of iron's behaviour. He termed these substances 'diamagnetics.' A bismuth rod suspended by a thin thread between the poles of a powerful electromagnet set itself equatorially (perpendicular to the line joining the poles), whereas an iron rod set itself axially. He measured the repulsive force on bismuth as approximately 1/3,400 of the attractive force on an equivalent volume of iron in the same field. Faraday concluded that diamagnetic substances acquire a magnetic polarity opposite in sense to the inducing field, in contrast to paramagnetics (like iron) whose induced polarity aligns with the field. He observed diamagnetism in bismuth, antimony, phosphorus, arsenic, and many other substances. This work is in Series XIX–XX (Volume III), not Volume I.",
    "source": "Experimental Researches in Electricity, Volume III (Series XIX–XX, 1845)",
    "concepts": ["diamagnetics", "bismuth", "equatorial orientation", "1/3400 of iron", "opposite polarity", "Series XIX Volume III"],
    "difficulty": "hard"
