  {
    "chunk_id": "faraday_028",
    "series": "Series VII",
    "paragraphs": "§872–874",
    "year": 1834,
    "topic": "Chemical Action Sustains Voltaic Current — Conclusion of Series VII",
    "text": "In §872, at the close of Series VII, Faraday drew a major conclusion: 'The definite production of electricity (§868.) in association with its definite action proves, I think, that the current of electricity in the voltaic pile is sustained by chemical decomposition, or rather by chemical action, and not by contact only.' This was a key intervention in the long-running debate about the source of voltaic electricity (contact theory vs. chemical theory). In §873 he qualified: 'I beg to reserve my opinion as to the real action of contact, not having yet been able to make up my mind as to whether it is an exciting cause of the current, or merely necessary to allow of the conduction of electricity, otherwise generated, from one metal to the other.' In §874 he closed with a vision: 'But who can doubt but that with increased knowledge of the proper conditions, and with an experimental investigation of the principles which govern the development and action of this subtile agent, we shall be able to increase the power of our batteries, or invent new instruments which shall a thousandfold surpass in energy those which we at present possess?'",
    "source": "Experimental Researches in Electricity, Volume I (Project Gutenberg #14986)",
